In high‑volume ambulatory endoscopy centers doing mostly low‑risk screening and surveillance procedures, it’s very common for an inpatient/ED‑oriented sedation bundle from a flagship hospital to feel far more intensive than what is workable or proportionate for every case. Those policies tend to be written after rare but serious events and with the sickest, highest‑acuity patients in mind, which is why they mandate universal in‑room anesthesia coverage, full cardiac monitoring plus capnography, long recovery periods, dense vital‑sign schedules, and strict discharge thresholds.

What your ambulatory sites have evolved toward—having GI physicians and experienced, sedation‑credentialed RNs manage moderate sedation independently for uncomplicated ASA I–II patients, reserving anesthesia presence and capnography for higher‑risk cases, using continuous pulse oximetry with less intensive cardiac monitoring in routine patients, and tailoring recovery length and vital‑sign frequency to how quickly a patient wakes and stabilizes—is similar to how many outpatient endoscopy programs pragmatically adapt those frameworks.

If you present the flagship policy to new staff as defining the upper bound of resources and monitoring that must be available for sicker or higher‑risk patients, and then explain that in routine ambulatory practice you scale up or down based on ASA class, comorbidities, observed response to sedation, and the complexity of the procedure, it can help reconcile what they see with what they’ve read. The emphasis can be on:

- ensuring robust initial screening and risk stratification (so true high‑risk patients do get anesthesia support, capnography, and longer, closely monitored recovery);
- maintaining consistent core safety practices for all (appropriate drug selection/dosing, continuous pulse oximetry, timely recognition of any airway or hemodynamic changes, clear discharge criteria and escort policies);
- keeping a low threshold to escalate monitoring or extend recovery when anything is borderline;
- and documenting the clinical rationale when routine, low‑risk cases are managed with a streamlined version of the flagship bundle.

Handled this way, your approach preserves the intent of the system’s sedation policy—preventing avoidable harm—without imposing the full inpatient‑style monitoring and recovery requirements on every healthy outpatient colonoscopy in a way that would make the service unsustainable.
